## Migration: EventProcessor (.NET Framework → .NET 8)

### Key Problems in the Original Code

| Issue | Why it breaks on .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — removed in .NET 5+ |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Still exists but unreliable; doesn't wake `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` |
| `ManualResetEvent` + manual `lock` + `Queue` | Race condition: multiple `Enqueue` calls before `Dequeue` loses items |
| No cancellation model | No clean shutdown path without `Abort` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _processingTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _processingTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (ct.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Clean shutdown — expected
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel is closed.");
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _processingTask is null) return;

        _channel.Writer.Complete();            // signal no more items
        _cts.CancelAfter(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // hard deadline

        await _processingTask;                 // wait for drain or cancellation
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

### What Changed and Why

**1. `Thread` → `Task.Run`** — Avoids dedicated thread; uses the thread pool. If you need a dedicated long-running thread, pass `TaskCreationOptions.LongRunning`.

**2. `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` + `Queue` → `Channel<T>`** — `Channel` is a thread-safe, async-ready producer/consumer queue built into .NET. It replaces *three* primitives with one, and fixes the race condition where multiple signals between `WaitOne`/`Reset` dropped items.

**3. `Thread.Abort()` / `Thread.Interrupt()` → `CancellationToken`** — Cooperative cancellation is the only supported model. The two-phase stop (`Complete()` then `CancelAfter`) gives the loop time to drain before forcing shutdown.

**4. `IAsyncDisposable`** — Ensures cleanup in `await using` blocks.

### Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---|---|
| **Swallowing all `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch when `ct.IsCancellationRequested` is true; otherwise rethrow — it may indicate a bug |
| **Using `BlockingCollection<T>` instead of `Channel<T>`** | `BlockingCollection` blocks threads; `Channel` is async-native and more efficient |
| **Forgetting `Writer.Complete()`** | `ReadAllAsync` will hang forever if you only cancel without completing the writer |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Always store the `Task` and `await` it during shutdown to observe exceptions |
| **Calling `Stop()` synchronously** | If you must have a sync `Stop()`, use `_processingTask.Wait(timeout)` — but prefer async |

### Usage

```csharp
await using var processor = new EventProcessor();
processor.Start();

processor.Enqueue("event-1");
processor.Enqueue("event-2");

await processor.StopAsync(); // drains remaining items, then stops
```
